\begin_layout Section
What Is Missing for Figures 6 to 8
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Figures 6, 7 and 8 plot, against the front time 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{f}$
\end_inset
 of the impulse, the inception voltage 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{cs}$
\end_inset
, the streamer length 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{cs}$
\end_inset
 and the charge 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{cs}$
\end_inset
 of the first and the second corona streamer. Each point is a separate impulse. The trends are clear: all three quantities fall as 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{f}$
\end_inset
 rises, and the second streamer always needs a higher voltage than the first. In Figures 3 to 5, 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{inc}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{cs}$
\end_inset
 were 
\series bold
inputs
\series default
; here they are 
\series bold
outputs
\series default
, so the model needs rules that produce them. None of these rules is given.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
An Inception Criterion That Depends on the Rate of Rise
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A fixed inception voltage (or a fixed tip field) gives the same 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{cs}$
\end_inset
 for every front, so it cannot produce the falling curve 1 of Figure 6. The fall comes from the 
\series bold
time lag
\series default
 of inception: after the tip field exceeds the static threshold, a free electron must appear in the high-field region (statistical lag) and the avalanche must grow into a streamer (formative lag). Meanwhile the voltage keeps rising at about 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{p}/T_{f}$
\end_inset
, so a fast front overshoots the static value more than a slow one.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Candidate model:
\series default
 a static threshold from the ionization integral along the axis,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\int_{0}^{x_{0}}\left[\alpha(E)-\eta(E)\right]dz\;\ge\;K,\qquad K\approx9\text{ to }18,\label{eq:ionization_integral}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta$
\end_inset
 are the ionization and attachment coefficients of air and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{0}$
\end_inset
 is where 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=\eta$
\end_inset
, plus a time lag after the threshold is first exceeded. The lag can be a fixed mean value, or a statistical waiting time fixed by the rate at which free electrons appear near the tip. The value of the lag is the main fitting parameter and would be calibrated on one point of curve 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
A Rule for the Streamer Length
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
 condition, 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{total}(L_{cs})=E_{c}$
\end_inset
, needs 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{cs}$
\end_inset
 as an input. With 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{cs}$
\end_inset
 unknown there are two unknowns and one condition, so a second, independent rule is needed. The paper's own assumption (a), that the first streamer develops in the applied field alone, suggests the standard stability-field estimate: the streamer grows until the 
\series bold
average applied field
\series default
 along its path falls to 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{c}$
\end_inset
,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{L_{cs}}\int_{0}^{L_{cs}}E_{ex}\left(z,t_{inc}\right)dz=\frac{V(t_{inc})-\varphi_{ex}(L_{cs})}{L_{cs}}=E_{c}.\label{eq:streamer_length}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A higher inception voltage gives a stronger field further from the tip and therefore a longer streamer, which is the trend of Figure 7. For the second streamer the same rule would use the total field, including the first streamer's charge. Before using it for Figures 6 to 8, the rule should be checked on the Figure 3 case: it should give a length close to 
\begin_inset Formula $2.7\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $27.5\,\text{kV}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Peak Voltage of Each Impulse
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The rate of rise, and with it the time lag effect, depends on the crest voltage 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{p}$
\end_inset
 as well as on 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{f}$
\end_inset
. The paper gives 
\begin_inset Formula $320\,\text{kV}$
\end_inset
 only for the 
\begin_inset Formula $80\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
 case of Figures 3 to 5. For the 
\begin_inset Formula $40\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
 gap of Figures 6 and 7 the crest is not stated, nor is the tail. A reasonable assumption is a crest near the breakdown voltage of the gap, the usual practice in such tests; the sensitivity of the results to this choice must then be shown.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Second Streamer
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Curve 2 of each figure needs the dark period and the second streamer. The ingredients exist from Figure 5: the drift of the first cloud under the rising voltage and the criterion of note 03 for the second inception. What is not given is how the second streamer's length and charge are found, and how the inception time lag applies to it. The natural choice is to reuse the inception and length rules above in the field that includes the first cloud.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Experimental Curves
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Figures 6 to 8 also show experimental curves, but the paper does not say where they come from (its own tests or reference [6], Arima and Watanabe, which uses a needle-plane gap). This matters when we compare our results with them.
\end_layout

